Pin openpty/forkpty/pthread_sigmask to their pre-merge glibc symbol versions.

Derived from orca's `config/patches/node-pty@1.1.0.patch` (MIT, Lovecast Inc. 2026),
reduced to the glibc-compatibility change; orca's patch also carries Windows and
node-addon-api edits this project does not need, since Windows is deferred.

Why this exists, from PHASE-5:

  glibc's 2.32-2.34 libpthread/libutil merge relocated three symbols node-pty uses:
  pthread_sigmask (GLIBC_2.32), openpty (GLIBC_2.34) and forkpty (GLIBC_2.34).
  Compile on a current CI runner and the loader refuses the result on Ubuntu 20.04 /
  Debian 11 / RHEL 9 with `version 'GLIBC_2.34' not found`. Orca shipped that and
  broke launch (their #9902).

The fix is two halves and needs both:

1. `.symver` pins each symbol to the version glibc still ships as a compat alias, so
   the object records GLIBC_2.2.5 (x86-64) or GLIBC_2.17 (aarch64) instead of the
   post-merge version.
2. `--no-as-needed` around `-l:libutil.so.1 -l:libpthread.so.0` keeps both in
   DT_NEEDED. Without it gcc's default `--as-needed` drops them, because at *build*
   time the pinned symbols resolve out of libc's own compat aliases — and then at run
   time on the older target there is nothing to resolve against.

`scripts/check-glibc-floor.mjs` is what proves the patch actually worked, because a
build with the patch silently not applied looks identical until it reaches an old
distro. CI runs it on every Linux slot.

diff --git a/binding.gyp b/binding.gyp
--- a/binding.gyp
+++ b/binding.gyp
@@ -60,6 +60,14 @@
               'libraries': [
                 '-lutil'
               ]
+            }],
+            # Pair with the .symver pins in src/unix/pty.cc. Force the real
+            # libutil.so.1/libpthread.so.0 into DT_NEEDED (gcc's default
+            # --as-needed drops them because the pinned symbols resolve from
+            # libc's compat aliases at build time) so openpty/forkpty/
+            # pthread_sigmask still resolve on Ubuntu 20.04 (glibc 2.31).
+            ['OS=="linux"', {
+              'ldflags': [
+                '-Wl,--no-as-needed,-l:libutil.so.1,-l:libpthread.so.0,--as-needed'
+              ]
             }]
           ]
         }
diff --git a/src/unix/pty.cc b/src/unix/pty.cc
--- a/src/unix/pty.cc
+++ b/src/unix/pty.cc
@@ -44,6 +44,24 @@
 #include <termios.h> /* tcgetattr, tty_ioctl */
 #endif

+/* glibc 2.32-2.34 relocated pthread_sigmask/openpty/forkpty into libc under new
+ * symbol versions, so building on a newer glibc produces references (GLIBC_2.32 /
+ * GLIBC_2.34) absent on Ubuntu 20.04 (glibc 2.31) and the addon fails to load.
+ * Pin these to the pre-merge version glibc still ships as a compat alias; the
+ * binding.gyp ldflags force libutil/libpthread into DT_NEEDED so those aliases are
+ * actually loaded on the target. */
+#if defined(__linux__) && defined(__GLIBC__)
+#  if defined(__x86_64__)
+#    define LEAP_CHORUS_GLIBC_COMPAT_VERSION "GLIBC_2.2.5"
+#  elif defined(__aarch64__)
+#    define LEAP_CHORUS_GLIBC_COMPAT_VERSION "GLIBC_2.17"
+#  endif
+#  ifdef LEAP_CHORUS_GLIBC_COMPAT_VERSION
+__asm__(".symver openpty,openpty@" LEAP_CHORUS_GLIBC_COMPAT_VERSION);
+__asm__(".symver forkpty,forkpty@" LEAP_CHORUS_GLIBC_COMPAT_VERSION);
+__asm__(".symver pthread_sigmask,pthread_sigmask@" LEAP_CHORUS_GLIBC_COMPAT_VERSION);
+#  endif
+#endif
+
 /* Some platforms name VWERASE and VDISCARD differently */
 #if !defined(VWERASE) && defined(VWERSE)
 #define VWERASE	VWERSE
